-*- coding:utf-8 -*-

autocad-no-rest-output-capture — probe princ output lost on AutoCAD (no-&rest path)
===================================================================================

Reported from: Windows vendor-probe job 15537669249
(vendor:probes:autocad, alfe 1.6.12), against AutoCAD 2022 accoreconsole.

The AutoCAD backend now works end to end EXCEPT output capture:

  * accoreconsole launches, `run.scr` runs, runtime bootstraps, READY 0 in
    ~0.8 s, the probe evaluates, status walks RUNNING 1 -> DONE 1 OK -> STOPPED,
    and alfe reports `plan finished status=:SUCCESS exit=0`. No error anywhere.
  * BUT the probe's printed output is GONE, so run-vendor-probes greps the log,
    finds no "ALL ENTITY PROBES PASSED", and reports FAIL -- "no verdict". This
    is a FALSE fail: the probe almost certainly passed, we just can't see it.

Kept-workdir evidence (alfe-autocad-11664-3q9m89):
    protocol/stdout.txt        0 bytes
    output.txt                 0 bytes
    errors.txt / stderr.txt    0 bytes
    accoreconsole .log         only `nil` (the eval result), then _QSAVE/_QUIT
    protocol/alfe-eval.lsp     (setq *AUTOLISP-EVAL-RESULT* (RUN-ENTITY-PROBES))
    debug.log                  "host lacks &rest; keeping bootstrap 1-arg shadows
                                + walk-rewriting normalize" ... server-loop
                                returned normally

Contrast: on BricsCAD (macOS + Windows) the SAME probe prints all 97 "ok" lines
+ "ALL ENTITY PROBES PASSED" and PASSES. BricsCAD logs "host supports &rest;
installing variadic shadows + no-op normalize". So the break is specific to the
**no-&rest** shadow/output path (AutoCAD lacks &rest; macOS BricsCAD has it).

The probe prints ONLY via 1-arg `(princ (strcat ...))` -- no &rest/&optional.
The loader (autolisp-source-load-run-body-impl) sets *AUTOLISP_CAPTURE_STDOUT* T
around each form's eval, so by inspection the top-level `(run-entity-probes)`
runs with capture on; emit-user-line/-out write to *AUTOLISP_OUTFILE* when
capture is T. Yet all sinks are empty -- the divergence is in the run-common
template's "host lacks &rest" branch (file-protocol.lisp, ~1240-1330: the princ
shadow install / OUTFILE binding), not in the probe.

Suspected root cause (under investigation): on the no-&rest path the in-effect
`princ` shadow either writes to a sink alfe never drains, or emit is bypassed,
or OUTFILE is not bound to protocol/stdout.txt. A subagent trace is pinpointing
the exact line.

Impact: AutoCAD conformance can't be *read* yet, though the engine runs the
probe cleanly. Lower priority than BricsCAD (which is GREEN both platforms).
pjb: "We may have to use automation when UI elements are tested, but we'll see
later" -- batch is fine for these probes; this is an output-capture bug, not a
UI-mode issue.

Root cause (confirmed) + fix (alfe 1.6.13)
------------------------------------------

On the no-&rest path the in-effect princ/print/prin1 shadows are the bootstrap's
FIXED 2-arg `(defun princ (obj file) ...)` (file REQUIRED; no &optional/&rest on
the fallback host). The walk-rewriter (autolisp-normalize-princ-call) is meant
to make user calls arity-safe, but it only rewrote the EMPTY `(princ)` case. So
a 1-arg `(princ "text")` raised "nombre d'arguments insuffisants" -- the SAME
trap that ate the runtime's own `(princ "")` earlier -- swallowed by the
enclosing vl-catch-all-apply, so the request still reported DONE 1 OK with nil
and zero bytes emitted. (On &rest hosts autolisp-normalize-princ-call is the
identity, so BricsCAD never used this and is unaffected.)

Fix (autolisp-bootstrap.lsp): extend the rewriter to pad ALL under-arity calls:
  * pre-check autolisp-form-contains-empty-princ-p now triggers on princ/print/
    prin1 with (length form) < 3 (0 or 1 args), not just empty princ.
  * autolisp-normalize-princ-call-impl: 1-arg `(princ X)`/`(print X)`/`(prin1 X)`
    -> pad the missing FILE with nil (`(princ X nil)`), arg walked too; empty
    `(princ)` keeps its newline alias; 2-arg calls pass through. Value contract
    preserved (princ returns its first arg). QUOTE/FUNCTION still stop the walk.

Also (1.6.14): `load` is shadowed with a fixed (file onfailure) arity on the
no-&rest host too (a vertical app's own load wrapper), so nested 1-arg
`(load "mod")` calls in app code hit the same trap. The rewriter now pads
`(load X)` -> `(load X nil)` as well (2-arg passes through). Matters for the
future app-context (EPURE) tests; the current probes don't call load internally.

Validated: both functions run in clautolisp (an AutoLISP interp) over sample
forms -- (princ "t")->(princ "t" nil), (load "m")->(load "m" nil), defun bodies
padded (both princ AND load), quoted forms untouched, 2-arg unchanged; alfe
rebuilt, clautolisp probes still 97/27/21, suite 306/306. BricsCAD unaffected
(normalize = identity there).

Status
------

FIX IN (1.6.13), OPEN pending the vendor:probes:autocad re-run -- expect the
probe output to now reach protocol/stdout.txt and the 97/27/21 verdicts to be
readable/green. The rewriter's cons-rebuild of princ-containing forms was a
known BricsCAD-V26 mapcar-lambda hazard, but it runs ONLY on the no-&rest
(AutoCAD) path; watch for any mapcar-lambda breakage there on the re-run.


Re-confirmed STILL REPRODUCING at HEAD (alfe 1.7.6, 2026-07-30, cad-triage-autocad)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Verified on the Windows runner against real AutoCAD 2022 accoreconsole:
  launching: .../AutoCAD 2022/accoreconsole.exe /i empty.dwg /s run.scr
  READY 0, plan finished status=:SUCCESS exit=0 -- and NO output.
A probe printing ONLY through 1-arg (princ (strcat ...)) (both -l and -x)
produced none of its markers (TRIAGE-OUT-A / TRIAGE-X-A / TRIAGE-DONE). So this
is NOT fixed by the 1.7.4 princ/prin1 newline work: that fix routes the &rest
VARIADIC shadows (BricsCAD has &rest); AutoCAD keeps the no-&rest 1-arg
bootstrap shadows + the walk-rewriter, and the output path there still drops
1-arg princ output.

Likely gap: the walk-rewriter (autolisp-normalize-princ-call) only pads the
TOP-LEVEL request form; 1-arg (princ X) calls INSIDE a loaded defun (the probe's
run-entity-probes, or any user function) are NOT rewritten, so on a host that
errors on under-arity they raise "too few arguments" and get swallowed. Fix
direction to try: make the bootstrap princ/print/prin1 shadows accept the file
arg via &optional (if accoreconsole's defun supports &optional) instead of
relying on the source-form rewriter — then any arity works without a walk. Needs
an AutoCAD triage of &optional support.

Side finding (separate ticket-worthy): the per-run workdir RANDOM tag is
CONSTANT -- alfe-autocad-<pid>-3q9m89 here AND in the original report -- so
workdir.lisp's random-tag is unseeded (only the PID varies). See
workdir-random-tag-unseeded (to file).


Root cause found + encoding half FIXED (alfe 1.7.7, verified on real AutoCAD 2022)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Deep diagnostics on real accoreconsole (cad-diag-autocad) showed the ticket's
"no-&rest arity" framing is WRONG:
  - the bootstrap DOES load and princ IS shadowed (AutoCAD 2022 reports every
    defun'd function as type SUBR, which earlier looked like "native princ");
  - the runtime DOES write the output to protocol/stdout.txt and alfe DOES
    drain it -- but the drained bytes came out as CJK mojibake.

The mojibake decodes (as UTF-16) back to the real text: the ENCODING was wrong.
alfe drained the protocol stdout/stderr FILES with accoreconsole's console
codec (:utf-16le, from the console-encoding work), but those files are written
by the AutoLISP runtime in UTF-8/ASCII -- a DIFFERENT channel from
accoreconsole's own console PIPE (which is UTF-16LE, decoded separately by
autocad-console-external-format). UTF-8 decoded as UTF-16 -> mojibake -> looked
like lost output.

FIX (this issue's branch fix-autocad-protocol-file-encoding):
%autocad-console-decode-encoding now defaults the FILE drain to the robust :AUTO
cascade (auto-detects real UTF-16LE via the interleaved NULs, else UTF-8/Latin-1)
and honours an explicit -Econsole/-Ecadstdio; the console PIPE stays :utf-16le.
VERIFIED on real AutoCAD 2022: a 1-arg (princ ...) inside a loaded defun now
drains CLEAN ASCII (PROBE-LINE-1 / PROBE-LINE-2 / PROBE-TOP), no mojibake.
SBCL 412/412.

REMAINING (keep this issue OPEN): the -x / eval-request path (autolisp-eval-
request-form's redefinition writes the form to alfe-eval.lsp and calls NATIVE
load) still drains "nil" lines instead of the form's princ output on
accoreconsole -- i.e. a form CALLED via a protocol request (as the original
vendor probe did: alfe-eval.lsp = "(setq *AUTOLISP-EVAL-RESULT* (RUN-ENTITY-
PROBES))") loses its output, while a form/defun loaded+run via -l (source-load)
is now correct. Next: make the eval-request native-load path capture the CAD
output (or route -x through source-load like -l), then re-verify the full
vendor probe on real AutoCAD.


== Capture design — pjb ruling (2026-08-02) ==

The variadic-shadow direction is DEAD: pjb confirms AutoCAD accepts NO variadic
mechanism at all — no &rest, no &optional, no other way to define a
variable-arity user function. So the fixed-arity shadow + source-form
walk-rewriter can never cover 1-arg princ calls made INSIDE a natively-loaded
defun (the probe's run-entity-probes). The earlier "&optional shadow" fix note
above is void.

Why we shadow princ/prin1/print at all: to capture their command-line output and
route it to alfe's REPL output. The clean analogue of CL's "bind *standard-
output* to another stream" is to REDIRECT the command-line stream itself, not
shadow the functions — via AutoCAD's command-history log:

  - Enable logging so 1-arg (princ x)/(prin1 x)/(print x) output is teed to a
    file, no file handle needed; let the NATIVE functions run (their real
    AutoCAD semantics preserved: bare (princ) -> newline; bare (print) -> does
    nothing, returns nil like (values)).
  - Sysvars (autolisp-spec + pjb): LOGFILEMODE is READ-ONLY (toggled by the
    LOGFILEON/LOGFILEOFF commands — but `command` is unusable under accoreconsole,
    so whether (setvar "LOGFILEMODE" 1) is even accepted MUST be tested);
    LOGFILEPATH = the DIRECTORY where per-drawing log files are stored (settable);
    LOGFILENAME = READ-ONLY path+name of the current drawing's log file.

Complication (pjb): user programs may ALSO use file logging for their own
purposes. So alfe must record whether logging is already active, TEE output to
both the user's log and alfe's capture, and if LOGFILEMODE/LOGFILEPATH are
changed mid-run, start / stop / retarget its tee accordingly. That requires
SHADOWING setvar to intercept those sysvar writes.

Open validation (pjb to run on a Windows or macOS accoreconsole): does
accoreconsole honour LOGFILEMODE / LOGFILEON and actually log a 1-arg
(princ "MARKER") to the LOGFILENAME file? If yes, implement the log-redirect +
setvar-shadow tee; if not, fall back to capturing accoreconsole's process stdout.

Related: [[accoreconsole-macos-headless-qt-blocker]] (a fresh macOS accoreconsole
run first has to get past its Qt::AA_PluginApplication bootstrap abort).
